PHASE 2: MAKE PRODUCTION DATA PERSISTENT

This is the highest-priority requirement.

Establish one authoritative production database.

If Neon PostgreSQL is the intended production database, make it the authoritative source for all persistent business records after verifying the existing database, schema, and data.

Do not assume that switching a connection string alone completes the migration.

Requirements:

- All production registration operations must write to the authoritative database.
- All login and account lookup operations must use the intended persistent identity store.
- All school, user, student, staff, invoice, payment, subscription, and reporting operations must use the correct persistent database.
- All application instances must access the same authoritative production records.
- Restarting the server must not remove data.
- Redeploying the application must not remove data.
- Scaling to another server instance must not create a separate set of user records.
- Logging out must not delete the account.
- Clearing a browser cache must not delete server-side business records.
- Installing or updating the PWA must not erase accounts or database records.
- Temporary database failures must not cause the application to initialize an empty replacement database.
- A failed save must never be reported to the user as a successful save.
- A failed database query must never be converted into a fabricated empty result.
- Database migrations must preserve existing records.
- Application startup must not automatically reset production data.
- Local development databases must never be used as silent production fallbacks.

If SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration is still incomplete, inspect and document the current state before migrating.

Create a safe, repeatable migration plan.

Before any production migration:

1. Verify a recent backup.
2. Record existing table names, row counts, and relevant integrity checks.
3. Inspect schema differences.
4. Map existing records to the target schema.
5. Preserve primary keys and relationships where required.
6. Handle duplicate records and conflicting identifiers explicitly.
7. Use transactions and appropriate migration safeguards.
8. Verify record counts and critical relationships after migration.
9. Keep the original data recoverable until migration acceptance.
10. Document the rollback procedure.

Never delete the original records simply because the migration script completed.

Remove dual-write behavior where it can cause inconsistent databases. Do not remove it until a safe transition has been implemented and verified.

Do not introduce any automatic database reset if a migration fails.

